FIX PAUSE-MENU HANG (810): edge bits read from a u16 -> always 0 -> infinite loop

Pause_Mode @0x446390 reads the pad as a 32-bit dword at 0x754448 and tests the
newly-pressed EDGE bits in its high word: `_DAT_00754448 & 0x100000` (up), 0x400000
(down), 0x10000 (select/fire), 0x800000/0x200000 (volume left/right) -- bits 16..23.
But 0x754448 is declared `unsigned short` (correct for every OTHER reader: the race
control code reads only the low-word button bits 0x1/0x10/0x40/0x80/0x20/0x1000/
0x4000/0x8, and the poll FUN_00422da4 writes the current-buttons word @0x754448 and
the edge word @0x75444a as two separate 16-bit stores). Masking a u16 with 0x100000
(bit 20) is ALWAYS 0, so none of the pause-menu navigation conditions ever fired: the
menu drew every frame but never responded to up/down/select and never returned ->
infinite loop. That is the "Pause-Menu bleibt haengen" hang. The pause ENTRY (Play_Game
@0x420c9c: `_DAT_0075444a & 8`) reads the edge word directly and so worked, which is
why pause could be entered but never exited.

The edge bits ARE present -- in the adjacent u16 _DAT_0075444a (image-contiguous high
half of the dword). Faithful fix: Pause_Mode reads/writes the true 32-bit dword via a
new `_pad_dword` accessor (*(unsigned int*)0x754448), exactly mirroring the asm
`mov eax,[0x754448]` / `mov dword [0x754448],eax`. FUN_00422da4 keeps its two 16-bit
half-stores (unchanged). int-typed-symbol / access-width class (ring patch, sun-y, etc).
Only Pause_Mode uses the high-word edge bits, so no other reader is affected.
